task: bench-stream-libraries
agent: opus-flink
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/bench-stream-libraries
worktree: ../okay-wt-lib-lanes

why: the operator asked for fs2, zio-streams and kyo in §20's table
  (2026-09-10). They belong there, but the claim they support is
  narrower than the section's others and must be stated before the
  numbers: NONE of the three has an event-time window (fs2's
  `groupWithin`, ZIO's `groupedWithin` are PROCESSING time; kyo has
  nothing), so each gets `okay.Windows` — the same operator okay's own
  lane and the JDK lane use — and what the numbers differ by is the
  library's plumbing around identical work.

what: `LibLanes.fs2 / .zio / .kyo`, one pass each, sources at a matched
  256-element granularity from each library's own chunked constructor
  (fs2 `Stream.chunk` + `chunkLimit`, PURE so no cats-effect runtime
  and no `unsafeRunSync`; `ZStream.fromChunk(...).rechunk(256)`; kyo
  `Stream.init(seq, 256)`). Same eleven checksums, asserted before any
  number is printed; three more rows in the whole-field table.

predict: all three land between the okay lane and the JDK's windowed
  collector — the work is the same and what is left is per-element
  delivery. If one of them is far off, the first thing to check is the
  source's real chunking in ITS OWN code (the rule that caught four
  mismatched lanes on 2026-09-06), not the library's honour.
